<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lithuanians are a Baltic people living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, who speak Lithuanian, one of the two surviving Baltic languages. Their tribes were united in the 13th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> became one of the largest states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They were among the last Europeans to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, in 1387, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>'s union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanians.jpg" alt="Lithuanians" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Lithuanian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
